{"reference":"2 Kings 17","book":"2 Kings","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter recounts Assyria’s capture of Samaria and the deportation of Israelites, then pauses to interpret that disaster as the outcome of sustained covenant disloyalty and rejection of prophetic warnings. Within 2 Kings, this fall is part of a larger movement from the ministries of Elijah and Elisha, through political upheaval and Assyrian conquest, toward Judah’s later reforms, Babylon’s destruction, and exile.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-23","2 Kings 17:1-41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_a2e53349adec72fb755b3db9","reader_path_2_kings_017_65642ae906765c6b3c5b3841"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Two Levels of Explanation","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter deliberately holds together two levels of explanation. It describes ordinary imperial actions—tribute, imprisonment, siege, deportation, and resettlement—while also explaining Israel’s fall in terms of its unfaithfulness to the covenant and its refusal of prophetic warnings. The theological interpretation does not erase the political events, and the political events do not replace the chapter’s own interpretation.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_50c8a8b1b1545b6dcb6a0f77"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"A Staged Assyrian Reduction","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The account should not be read as though every loss, siege, deportation, and incoming settlement happened in one simple operation. Second Kings distributes these developments across several notices. Its wider sequence also portrays tribute in different settings: it can follow defeat, strategic submission, an appeal for help, or renewed vassalage. Thus Hoshea’s tribute and later refusal of tribute describe a particular stage in the narrative rather than a complete explanation of his motives.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-41","2 Kings 17:1-6","2 Kings 17:24-41","2 Kings 17:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_df39e589d2261aa2fea6d2b0"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading Deportation and Resettlement","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The removals from Samaria and the arrival of new populations fit a recognizable pattern of Assyrian imperial administration, including forced transfers and the reorganization of conquered territory. That context helps clarify the setting of verses 24–41 without reducing the passage to administrative history: the chapter’s own concern remains the relationship among land, population, and worship. Assyrian royal claims about Samaria are useful near-contemporary witnesses to this conquest horizon, but they should not simply be merged with this account, since their chronology, agency, and genre differ.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-6","2 Kings 17:24-41","2 Kings 17:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_a86abbbd6fbace686c667e71"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"What Deportation Does—and Does Not—Mean","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The deportation was coercive and disruptive, but it should not be imagined as the literal disappearance of every northern Israelite. The available evidence supports removal, survival, provincial resettlement, and continuing identities. Likewise, a deportee total in a royal inscription is a royal claim with variant figures, not an exact census of all who were removed from the northern kingdom.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-41","2 Kings 17:1-6","2 Kings 17:24","2 Kings 17:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_ae165df46b02c84de15353d9"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"chronology","title":"Historical Horizon","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"A debated chronological orientation places Samaria’s fall in 722/721 BCE. It also distinguishes that event from Sennacherib’s later 701 BCE campaign in Judah, preserving two separate Assyrian horizons rather than collapsing them into one.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 17:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_017_b0e84ffe01375cb061566650"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}